// =============================================================================
// Base.xcconfig — settings shared by every build configuration.
//
// IMPORTANT: nothing in this file is secret. Build settings referenced from
// Info.plist end up in the shipped app bundle, where anyone can read them with
// `plutil -p YourApp.app/Contents/Info.plist`. Treat every value here as public.
//
// OAuth client IDs are public identifiers by design. The flow is protected by
// PKCE (see PKCE.swift), not by keeping the client ID hidden. Client *secrets*
// must never appear here, or anywhere else in the app.
//
// Per-developer overrides live in Config/Local.xcconfig, which is gitignored.
// Copy Config/Local.xcconfig.example to Config/Local.xcconfig to get started.
// That file is included at the BOTTOM of this one so its values win over the
// placeholder defaults below.
// =============================================================================


// ── Google OAuth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Register the client in Google Cloud Console as application type "iOS" — it
// covers macOS apps too. Do NOT use type "Desktop app": those are issued a
// client secret that Google's token endpoint then requires, which would force a
// secret into the app bundle. iOS-type clients have no secret and use PKCE.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID = UNSET.apps.googleusercontent.com

// Google iOS clients only accept a redirect URI whose scheme is the client ID
// in reverse-DNS form, e.g.
//   com.googleusercontent.apps.123456789012-abcdefghijklmnop
// An arbitrary scheme such as com.retro.dailytracker will be rejected with
// redirect_uri_mismatch.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_SCHEME = com.googleusercontent.apps.UNSET

// EXACTLY ONE SLASH after the scheme. This is not a typo, and it differs from the
// Microsoft URI below.
//
// Google's native clients require the custom-scheme redirect to have no
// authority component:
//     com.googleusercontent.apps.IDENTIFIER:/oauth2redirect     <- correct
//     com.googleusercontent.apps.IDENTIFIER://oauth2redirect    <- rejected
//
// With `//`, `oauth2redirect` parses as the URI authority (host) and the path is
// empty. With `/` it is the path and there is no authority. Google rejects the
// authority form with:
//     Error 400: invalid_request ... flowName=GeneralOAuthFlow
//
// See https://github.com/openid/AppAuth-iOS/blob/master/Examples/README-Google.md
// which notes there is only a single slash after the scheme.
//
// A single `/` is not a comment in xcconfig, so no `$()` escaping is needed here.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI = $(GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_SCHEME):/oauth2redirect


// ── Microsoft OAuth (Entra ID / Graph) ───────────────────────────────────────
// Register under "Mobile and desktop applications" as a public client. Public
// clients cannot have a client secret, and Entra ID rejects one if sent.
//
// The placeholder deliberately contains the literal `UNSET` rather than a
// plausible-looking dummy GUID. AppConfig rejects any value containing `UNSET`,
// so a value that was never filled in fails fast with a clear message instead of
// reaching Entra ID and coming back as an opaque invalid_client error.
MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID = UNSET-MICROSOFT-CLIENT-ID

// Microsoft accepts a custom reverse-DNS scheme for native redirects, so this
// one can stay aligned with the bundle identifier.
MICROSOFT_OAUTH_REDIRECT_SCHEME = com.retro.dailytracker

// Note the contrast with Google above: Entra ID accepts the conventional `://`
// authority form for custom schemes, matching the `msauth.<bundle-id>://auth`
// pattern MSAL uses. This value must match the redirect registered in the portal
// character for character.
//
// The `$()` here IS required: `//` starts a comment in xcconfig files, so writing
// `scheme://path` directly would silently truncate the value to `scheme:`. `$()`
// expands to nothing and breaks up the `//` token so both slashes survive.
MICROSOFT_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI = $(MICROSOFT_OAUTH_REDIRECT_SCHEME):/$()/oauth/callback


// ── Local, gitignored overrides ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// `#include?` (with the question mark) is the optional form: the build succeeds
// when the file is absent, which keeps a fresh clone buildable.
#include? "Local.xcconfig"
